== 6.1 Track and Field Events

*Athletics* includes a range of running, jumping and throwing events. Track events commonly include sprints, middle- and long-distance races, hurdles and relays. Field events include long jump, high jump, shot put, discus, javelin and other jumps or throws. Road races and combined events are also part of the wider sport; the venue and programme depend on the event.

A *sprint* is a short, fast race; a distance race requires pacing over a longer course. The specific distances and race rules depend on the competition. Hurdle races involve clearing barriers while running. Skill, preparation and safe facilities are important in all events.

#rule-box("Track or field?")[
  A race on the track is a track event. Jumping and throwing events are generally field events. Identify what the athlete is doing before choosing the category.
]

== 6.2 Starts, Relays and Running Skills

Runners use techniques suited to the race. Sprint starts may use starting blocks in events where the rules require or permit them; longer races may use a standing start. The start procedure and consequences of an infringement follow the applicable competition rules and officials' instructions.

A *relay* involves team members running successive portions and passing a baton. The exchange must comply with the event's rules, including the designated exchange area. A successful exchange combines communication, timing and safe movement; do not assume that a memorised exchange-area measurement applies to every event or every version of the rules.

